  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 246 PM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nOn Monday morning, southeast Wyoming and western Nebraska will \nsit in-between a surface low to the east in the Great Plains and\nan upper-level trough to the west over the northern Rockies. \nDespite the presence of nearby systems however, the day will \nlikely start off mostly dry given a lack of favorable overlap of\nlift and moisture directly in our local vicinity. As Monday \nprogresses, the upper-level trough to the west will swing \neastward, and surface winds will shift out of the north. \nLingering low-level moisture to the northwest of the surface low\nlooks to get wrapped around the trough as it departs, \nincreasing the chances of showers and possibly some snow showers\nto east-central Wyoming and potentially the NE panhandle. \nEnsemble QPF totals with the backside of this system on Monday \nafternoon/night still remain rather low and dispersive, similar \nto overnight guidance. Overall, it looks like only a tenth of an\ninch or so of QPF is likely east of I-25 and north of I-80. \nHigher amounts are possible in the northern Laramie range where \nfavorable orographics and snowfall may last a bit longer. The \nother big story on Monday and Monday night will be a dramatic \nincrease in winds on the backside of this system in the wake of \na passing cold front. Ensemble guidance still is in strong \nsupport of winds possibly exceeding 50 mph, even approaching \nhigh wind criteria east of I-25 through the NE panhandle. The \nstrongest period of high winds appears to be mid to pate \nafternoon, potentially over the I-80 corridor, as mixing is \nmaximized at the time of the passage of the strongest mid-level \njet aloft. The north to north-northwesterly orientation of \nsurface flow could bring significant crosswind impacts to \ntravelers along I-80 from the summit eastward through the high \nplains during this timeframe.\n \nAs the high wind even diminishes on Tuesday morning, dry weather\nand above-average temperatures will move in for the remainder of\nthe week. The synoptic pattern aloft does remain fairly active,\nwith two weak vort-maxes passing by on Tuesday night and on\nWednesday night. However, both of these systems will be starved\nof moisture and bring only occasional increases in mid and\nupper-level cloud cover through the area. Forecast confidence\nremains relatively high through the end of the week on both\nabove normal temperatures and drier weather. However, by the end\nof the week, attention will turn to a larger system with upper-\nlevel support moving through the southwestern CONUS. Ensemble\nguidance is quite dispersive with both the track and the amount\nof moisture with this event, but there is a broad consensus that\nWyoming and possibly western Nebraska will see some form of\nprecipitation from this event given its more favorable storm\ntrack bringing in southerly and southeasterly flow. We will\ncontinue to monitor this system and potential impacts with both\nprecipitation and winter weather in the high country as we start\nthe month of November.",
